  "image_name": "5336242c69dda3359e6a665fa7e119f0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a standard photon-correlation measurement and the beam-splitter behavior behind it. In the top schematic, a stream of single photons is sent to a 50:50 beamsplitter, which randomly directs each photon either to a transmitted path or a reflected path. Two detectors monitor these outputs, labeled as start and stop channels, and the timing difference between their clicks is used to build the second-order correlation function, \\(g^{(2)}(\\tau)\\). The equations indicate that detector counts are proportional to the light intensity at time \\(t\\) and at a delay \\(t+\\tau\\), and the coincidence rate is normalized by the average intensity squared.\n\nThe lower cartoons show what happens when two single photons arrive at the beamsplitter from different input ports. The possible outputs shown are that both photons leave together in one output arm or together in the other arm, while the case where they split and exit separately is crossed out. Visually, this emphasizes two-photon interference: indistinguishable photons bunch at the beamsplitter, eliminating coincidence events between the two detectors. Overall, the figure connects coincidence detection with the quantum behavior of single photons at a beamsplitter.",
